WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.000
Mosquitoes are not just a nuisance,

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.920
but the carriers of disease
like Zika, dengue fever.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.480
DEET has been the go-to
repellent for decades now.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.520
So what does research say about
its safety and effectiveness?

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.920
And are there better,
natural alternatives?

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.100
I'll answer these questions
in this video and the next.

00:00:19.224 --> 00:00:29.076
"Repelling Mosquitoes - Is DEET the Best?
What About Natural Alternatives?"

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.120
The most dangerous animal in
the world isn't the Great White,

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.520
or the King Cobra, or lions
and tigers and bears.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.360
In fact, only about 10 people die
in shark attacks every year.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.320
Coming in #2 as most dangerous?

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.080
Fellow human beings.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.680
But the worst?

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.120
Mosquitoes.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.040
Literally billions of people
are at risk

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.200
of contracting dengue fever
from mosquitoes,

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:58.240
and hundreds of thousands die from
mosquito-borne malaria every year.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.920
New threats like Zika continue
to pose a global public health threat,

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.120
such that the World Health Organization
suggested delaying pregnancy

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.920
in Zika-affected areas
around the world.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.640
What's the best mosquito
repellent to wear?

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.160
There are products like permethrin,

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.360
a product originally derived from
chrysanthemums interestingly,

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.600
that can be applied to clothes,

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.960
but what about repellents
you actually put on your skin?

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.440
DEET is the repellent to beat,
considered the gold standard

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:28.320
of protection when it's
crucial not to get bitten.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.840
It was developed back in the
1940s for use by the military.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.440
It's long been considered
the first-line mosquito repellent.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.800
Effectiveness-wise 20% to 50%
DEET repellents

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.600
provide up to several
hours of protection.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.680
That's rubbed on the skin, though.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.680
DEET-impregnated wristbands
don't work, repelling mosquitoes

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.760
only from areas
covered by the band,

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.920
which I guess you could say about
non-DEET-impregnated wristbands.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.480
DEET or citronella wristbands
have been clocked at working

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.240
more broadly for only
12 to 18 seconds.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.560
Safety-wise, DEET
is considered safe

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.480
even in the second
and third trimesters

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:05.280
of pregnancy,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.560
and in children as long as
they're older than two months.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.760
Now it should be noted that
DEET can damage plastics

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.200
and synthetic materials;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.360
therefore, care should
be taken when it's used

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.360
around plastic watches, eyeglasses,
and synthetic fabrics.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.960
Nylon is okay, but it's been found
to damage spandex, rayon, acetate,

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.960
and pigmented leather,
in addition to plastic and vinyl.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.600
DEET is absorbed through your
skin into your bloodstream,

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.640
but it's cleared from your system
within a few hours.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.800
Does it have any adverse effects?

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.160
DEET is probably far less toxic
than many people believe.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.680
DEET has a remarkable safety profile
after now more than a half century

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.760
of use and billions of applications.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.440
Fewer than 50 cases of serious
toxic effects have been documented

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.680
in the medical literature since 1960,
and most of them resolved.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.760
Most reported cases of adverse
or lethal events involved overuse

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.480
or incorrect use of the product --

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.800
incorrect use as in chugging
it to commit suicide.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.200
What's a correct usage?

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.520
Read and follow all directions
on the product label.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.640
Only apply to intact
non-irritated skin.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.560
Do not apply near
eyes and mouth

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.800
and only sparingly
around ears.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.320
That's to avoid accidental
eye exposure or ingestion.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.000
It can be applied to the face,
but don't spray it in your face.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.480
Spray it on your hands
and then you can dab on,

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.680
but still avoid the eyes and mouth.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.080
Apply it to children
so they don't swallow any.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.480
Just use enough to
cover exposed skin

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.880
or on the outside of clothing,
not underneath.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.760
And then once you're back inside,
wash it off with soap and water,

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.000
and wash any treated clothing.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.560
If you do have a reaction,
stop using it, wash it off,

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:44.720
call your local poison control center.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.520
And if you go to your doctor,
bring the bottle.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.480
So which mosquito repellent
works best?

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.240
Well, in head-to-head tests,
DEET crushed it.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.360
But this was in reference to a study
published about 20 years ago.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.520
Anything new on the market that
won't melt your eyeglasses?

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.320
We'll find out next.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:10.560
DEET has been considered the most
effective mosquito repellent.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.240
Unquestionably, read this editorial
in The Journal of Family Practice,

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.880
it should be the only
mosquito repellent recommended

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:20.480
by physicians, with no other
repellent coming close.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:22.000
Given the dramatic efficacy,

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.000
it's hard to conceive that any
other repellent would ever beat it.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.320
However, there are some rare reports
of severe reactions to DEET,

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.000
not the mention the fact that it can
melt plastics, like eyeglass frames

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.440
and cellphone components,

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.120
and many consumers find the odor
and sensation on the skin unpleasant.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.600
Enter picaridin. Overall, studies
have shown little difference

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.400
between DEET and picaridin
applied at the same dosage,

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:50.320
with some evidence pointing to
a superior persistence for picaridin,

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:54.720
all without the irritancy, odor,
and melted glasses.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.200
No wonder it got
Consumer Reports pick

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.600
for the best overall insect repellent.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.200
Note that concentration matters.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.920
Their 20% picaridin product
topped the list,

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:07.600
but at 5% it was one of their
worst performing products.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.880
Any toxicity?
Adverse effects, when occurring,

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.600
primarily manifested as
eye irritation/redness,

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.840
vomiting, and oral irritation,

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.360
but of course you're not supposed
to eat it or spray it in your eyes.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.320
But even unintentional ingestion
was associated

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.960
with only relatively minor toxicity.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.480
What about the electronic
mosquito repellent gizmos?

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.600
Ten studies done, and all ten found
that there was no difference

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.440
in the number of mosquitoes landing
on people with or without the gizmos

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.760
and experiments out
in the field confirm:

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.960
no effect on preventing
mosquito bites.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:44.320
Picaridin was roughly based
on a black pepper compound,

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.640
but like DEET is
a synthetic chemical.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.640
Are there any natural repellents?

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:50.560
Of course, just because
something is natural

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:52.800
does' t mean it's necessarily safe.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.920
Strychnine is a natural product
of the strychnine tree,

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.560
and ricin from castor beans
is 10,000 times deadlier still.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:05.760
In fact, the top 10 most toxic
poisons are all natural.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.320
But let's look at the mosquito
repellent effects

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.760
of about 20 essential oils
compared to a placebo

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.920
control group and to DEET.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.480
The asterisks point
to the significant results,

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:18.240
so here's a cleaner peek.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.760
As you can see, only five
had any lasting effect at all.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:24.960
Peppermint and lemongrass oil
were effective for 30 minutes.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.040
Spearmint and garlic oil
started working

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:28.800
but didn't even last that long.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.120
Cinnamon oil, though,
reduced mosquito attraction

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:32.640
for one and a half hours.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:35.520
The remaining essential oils
had no significant effect

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.560
on mosquito attraction at any time
point, and this includes citronella.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:44.240
Citronella was the most widely used
repellent before DEET was invented,

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:46.480
and is still used today
in many formulations

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:48.880
despite inferior efficacy.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:52.000
At lower concentrations,
it may only last a few minutes,

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.000
and at higher concentrations
citronella can cause skin irritation.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.760
Compared to a complete protection
time of 6 hours for DEET,

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:02.480
citronella may only
last 10-1/2 minutes.

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:04.480
Therefore, citronella
may be acceptable

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:07.440
for brief exposure
to nuisance mosquitoes,

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:12.240
but it is not advised for protection
if you really can't afford to get bit.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.120
Essential oils, read this family
medicine journal editorial,

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.640
have no effectiveness
and are not recommended.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:22.960
But that was before we learned
about lemon eucalyptus,

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:27.120
the only plant-based repellent
recommended by the CDC,

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.040
though should not be used
by pregnant women

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:32.240
or children younger
than three years of age.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:35.040
Consumer Reports listed it
as one of their top three picks,

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:38.720
warding off mosquitoes and ticks
for at least seven hours.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:41.760
All the other botanical products
they tested failed.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:45.680
But 40% lemon eucalyptus was shown
to prevent bites for 4 to 7 hours

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:48.200
after application for aggressive
species of mosquitos

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:51.840
and for greater than 12 hours
for less aggressive mosquitoes,

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.927
a period of prevention greater than
at least a 10% DEET repellent.

